        <h2 id="next-title">Where it's going</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Today driftlab injects real faults into real processes, so a rare failure can take many runs to show up, and a timing bug may not repeat. The aim is deterministic testing: one seed replays the same schedule and the same faults every time, so a failure found once can be replayed until it's fixed.</p>
        <ul class="todo">
          <li><span class="when">Now</span><span>Any language: the runner and the checks split from the Go drivers, starting with the Java and Python Kafka clients, through the same five faults.</span></li>
          <li><span class="when">Next</span><span>A second Kafka calibration: driftlab has to catch a known franz&#8209;go message-loss bug on the version before its fix.</span></li>
          <li><span class="when">Then</span><span>Ledger checks you can reuse: every confirmed transfer applied exactly once, money conserved, a retry never applied twice. Blnk first.</span></li>
          <li><span class="when">After that</span><span>RabbitMQ: amqp091&#8209;go's automatic recovery, added in 2026, which reconnects and sets its consumers up again when the connection drops.</span></li>
          <li><span class="when">Later</span><span>Deterministic runs, where one seed replays the whole schedule.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
